  "description": {
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (en): Commiphora kua",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commiphora_kua",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 1178143934,
      "text": "Commiphora kua, sometimes known as Abyssinian myrrh or the Yemen myrrh, is a plant native to northeast Africa and the Arabian Peninsula, including Djibouti, Eritrea, Ethiopia, Zambia, Malawi, Oman and Yemen. It was first described as Balsamodendrum kua in 1847, and has many botanical synonyms. It can be recognised by its simple, serrate leaves and by the pseudo aril, covering the seed, which has four almost linear arm-like lobes."
    },
    "fr": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (fr): Commiphora kua",
      "source_url": "https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commiphora_kua",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-14",
      "revision": 239040284,
      "text": "Commiphora kua est une espèce de plantes à fleurs de la famille des Burseraceae. Son aire de répartition naturelle s'étend de l'Érythrée à la Zambie, en passant par le sud de la péninsule arabique. Il s'agit d'un arbuste ou d'un arbre qui pousse principalement dans les biomes désertiques ou les zones arbustives sèches."
    }
  },
